The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-4&2&-3\\-2&-1&-4\\4&-2&k\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&-4\\-2&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-4\\4&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(-3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-1\\4&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-4)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (k)-(-4)\cdot (-2))\\&\quad -(2)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (k)-(-4)\cdot (4))\\&\quad +(-3)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (-2)-(-1)\cdot (4))\\&=(-4)\cdot (- k - 8)\\&\quad -(2)\cdot (16 - 2 k)\\&\quad +(-3)\cdot (8)\\&=8 k - 24
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
8 k - 24&=0\\8 k&=24\\k&=3
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=3$.

