\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-2&4&k\\-1&-1&0\\0&-4&k\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&0\\-4&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(4)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&0\\0&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-1&-1\\0&-4\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-2)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (k)-(0)\cdot (-4))\\&\quad -(4)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (k)-(0)\cdot (0))\\&\quad +(k)\cdot ((-1)\cdot (-4)-(-1)\cdot (0))\\&=(-2)\cdot (- k)\\&\quad -(4)\cdot (- k)\\&\quad +(k)\cdot (4)\\&=10 k
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
10 k&=0\\10 k&=0\\k&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=0$.

